WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:06.693
Python's standard library does not include a balanced binary search tree like C++ std::set.

00:00:06.693 --> 00:00:14.931
When updates and order-statistic queries interleave, naive list insertions degrade to linear time per operation.

00:00:14.931 --> 00:00:20.080
We emulate an ordered set using a Fenwick tree or square-root buckets.

00:00:20.367 --> 00:00:27.032
If all possible keys are known ahead of time, sort and deduplicate them into compressed ranks.

00:00:27.032 --> 00:00:33.130
Maintain a Fenwick tree of counts where inserting adds one and deleting subtracts one.

00:00:33.130 --> 00:00:39.087
Prefix queries immediately report how many active elements are smaller than any key.

00:00:39.367 --> 00:00:45.658
To find the k-th smallest active element, binary-lift directly down the Fenwick tree array.

00:00:45.658 --> 00:00:50.981
Step by decreasing powers of two whenever the cumulative count stays below k.

00:00:50.981 --> 00:00:56.927
This single logarithmic walk avoids an extra factor of log N from outer binary search.

00:00:57.200 --> 00:01:04.474
When keys cannot be known offline, partition the sorted elements into blocks of size roughly square root of N.

00:01:04.474 --> 00:01:10.491
Locate the target block and position with bisect, and split any block that grows too large.

00:01:10.491 --> 00:01:13.731
This sorted-list design runs fast in pure Python.
